Offer the Voodoo 3 / Banshee post filter on a Voodoo 2 (doc 21 §13).

A 3dfx card of this era draws into a 16-bit frame buffer with an ordered
dither and takes the dither back out in the RAMDAC on the way to the
monitor -- "22-bit colour". 86Box has three of those filters: one for the
Voodoo Graphics, one for the Voodoo 2 (both in this file, and the Voodoo
2's is what `-device voodoo2,filter=v2` already picks), and a third,
later and better-tuned pair written for the Banshee and Voodoo 3 -- a 4x1
and a 2x2 -- which live in vid_voodoo_banshee.c, a file this port does
not vendor (voodoo/86box/UPSTREAM: a Banshee is a 2D+3D card with its own
VGA core, and nothing else of it is reachable for a Voodoo 2).

Those two are ported into voodoo/voodoo_vbfilter.c, ours, same licence:
voodoo_generate_vb_filters() verbatim -- this file already declares and
calls it, and the shim used to stub it -- and voodoo_filterline_vb(),
which is banshee_render_line()'s two filtered cases with the overlay, the
chroma key and the scaling taken out, in the shape voodoo_filterline_v1
and _v2 have.

What is left is the call site, which is here, and it is picked the way
the other two are: `voodoo->scrfilter` is the device's `filter=` property
and no longer just on or off -- 1 is the card's own filter, 2 the Voodoo
3's 4x1, 3 its 2x2 -- so the display timer chooses between three filters
instead of two, and voodoo_threshold_check() builds the Banshee tables
for a Voodoo 2 that was asked for them as well as for a Banshee.

The file is 86Box's, vendored verbatim in voodoo/86box and overlaid into
hw/voodoo by prepare-qemu.sh, so the change is a patch on the overlaid
copy (like patch 64) rather than an edit: scripts/sync-86box-voodoo.sh
keeps working, and this is dropped the day the Banshee sources are
vendored or 86Box offers its filters to a Voodoo 2 itself.

diff --git a/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_display.c b/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_display.c
index b126fc1..e80c3c8 100644
--- a/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_display.c
+++ b/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_display.c
@@ -38,6 +38,14 @@
 #include <86box/vid_voodoo_regs.h>
 #include <86box/vid_voodoo_render.h>
 
+/* 2ksbox: the Voodoo 3 / Banshee post filter over this card's scanlines
+ * (voodoo/voodoo_vbfilter.c, doc 21 §13) -- scrfilter 2 is its 4x1
+ * filter, the one 3dfx sold as 22-bit colour, and 3 its 2x2. Declared
+ * here because the file that has it is ours, not 86Box's: the Banshee
+ * sources these two filters come from are not vendored. */
+void voodoo_filterline_vb(voodoo_t *voodoo, uint8_t *fil, int column,
+                          uint16_t *src, int line, int mode);
+
 #ifdef ENABLE_VOODOODISP_LOG
 int voodoodisp_do_log = ENABLE_VOODOODISP_LOG;
 
@@ -379,7 +387,7 @@ voodoo_threshold_check(voodoo_t *voodoo)
         else
             voodoo_generate_filter_v1(voodoo);
 
-        if (voodoo->type >= VOODOO_BANSHEE)
+        if (voodoo->type >= VOODOO_BANSHEE || voodoo->scrfilter >= 2)
             voodoo_generate_vb_filters(voodoo, FILTCAP, FILTCAPG);
     }
 }
@@ -555,7 +563,10 @@ voodoo_callback(void *priv)
                     uint8_t fil[4096 * 3]; /* interleaved 24-bit RGB */
 
                     assert(voodoo->h_disp <= 4096);
-                    if (voodoo->type == VOODOO_2)
+                    if (voodoo->scrfilter >= 2)
+                        voodoo_filterline_vb(voodoo, fil, voodoo->h_disp, src,
+                                             voodoo->line, voodoo->scrfilter - 1);
+                    else if (voodoo->type == VOODOO_2)
                         voodoo_filterline_v2(voodoo, fil, voodoo->h_disp, src, voodoo->line);
                     else
                         voodoo_filterline_v1(voodoo, fil, voodoo->h_disp, src, voodoo->line);
